19
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:48,960
Oh, I'm glad you asked.

20
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:51,600
One of the biggest things that I hear.

21
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,400
a lot from, and you said CEOs and maybe product managers and people who don't necessarily do ASO, because that's how you do ASO.

22
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:03,600
If you think about it, and something that I get asked a lot is, should I care about that to begin with?

23
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:05,760
And they ask me because they don't know what it is.

24
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,360
I think that's really what it is.

25
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:12,960
So many people say ASO, ASO, ASO, and some people say SEO, and we have a SEO team, so do we need ASO?

26
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,720
And that's something I guess we'll talk about later.

27
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:27,600
But at the end of the day, you need discovery, and that's what should matter to everyone, whether you own the company, you're the founder, you're the CEO, you run the company, or if you're responsible for a product, whether it's marketing or a product or really anything else.

28
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:35,920
And that's where ASO comes in, that discovery, the ability to get found in the App Store, that's what's becoming harder and harder and harder to get.

29
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:43,760
And in the old days, the days when there were fewer apps and a lot of really interested users, you would get it a lot more easily.

30
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:52,640
And the way the App Store was, would lend itself to seeing more apps that are out there, not necessarily the curated, because curation didn't happen until fairly recently.

31
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:54,400
Today it's a lot more difficult.

32
00:02:54,640 --> 00:03:13,280
So one of the main ways to get found on the App Store is someone comes in and they look for something, something that they have some sort of intent, and you come up as a result for that int, you need app store optimization because you need to help the algorithm see what it is that your app does because it just doesn't know.

33
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,440
And that's really the base of everything, the way I see it.

34
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:22,000
You want discovery, search is a good way to get discovered and you need to help the algorithm.

35
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:32,320
How do you see the blend of ASO as that discovery engine and then ASO as optimizing for the black box that is the app store?

36
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:35,840
I mean, to get an app, you have to go to the app store.

37
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:48,400
So some people will just go search a branded term like Pinterest or something, but Pinterest still needs to think about ASO.O even when people are searching their brand name because the screenshots still need to be engaging, the title and subtitle?

38
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:57,040
So how do you think about that blend of optimizing for ads that are sending people to the store, brand searches that are sending people to the store?

39
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:05,920
So there's kind of that side of ASO that is optimizing toward conversion, and then there's a side of SEO that is optimizing toward discovery.

40
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,160
How do you think about blending those two?

41
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:19,440
So when I think about it, I think about how do you optimize for people more so than the algorithm more so than for discovery more so than conversion because ultimately they're all fairly the same.

42
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:23,120
If you think about it, and there was a I believe an article by John Gruber.

43
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:30,560
So there was an article about how I believe it was Dropbox was using App Store optimization and was saying something about photos in their name.

44
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:36,880
And his whole point was that this app is about more things than just photos and they should just be called Dropbox.

45
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:51,200
And I had a problem with that because at the end of the day, if my mom comes into the App Store and she's looking for a way to store her photos because maybe she lost her iPhone once and they all disappeared and she really wants to see her grandchildren, she needs to have an app to do that.

46
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:55,520
If she goes into the App Store and she sees Dropbox, she's not going to make that connection in her head necessarily.

47
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:55,920
I will.

48
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:00,560
You, and maybe some or most of the viewers will, but not necessarily.

49
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:07,040
And so when you optimize, you're not just helping the algorithm see that this app can help with photo storage.

50
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:11,600
You also see that as a human, this app will help me with photo storage.

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,320
And that's kind of the same at the end of the day.

52
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:22,000
There are a lot of little things that you need to know for the algorithm, but those are usually not the kind of things that take away from optimizing for people.

53
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,280
Those are the kind of things that you will do in the background.

54
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,040
Yeah, that's a great way to think about it.

55
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:34,800
It's like you can optimize for both simultaneously because you're ultimately just optimizing for people better understanding the app and seeing some value in it.

56
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:35,520
And that's great.

57
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:41,040
And then just briefly, what is the difference between ASO and SEO?

58
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:43,040
I mean, people are so familiar with SEO.

59
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:45,440
What is ASO and what makes it different?

60
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:56,000
I don't know if briefly can be done, but search engine optimization is taking your website and optimizing it for Google in most cases.

61
00:05:56,560 --> 00:06:03,200
So Google will come and read all of your pages and will try to understand what they're about so they can float them for relevant search queries.

62
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,680
App Store optimization on the other hand, you don't really have pages in that same sense.

63
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:19,040
You don't have control over the content that you have on is just one page with three ish elements that you have in control.

64
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:20,800
So it's totally different.

65
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:33,520
Although at the core, the ideas are somewhat similar, and that's why some companies, especially the enterprise, believe that their SEO guy or SEO person can easily do ASO, and they usually see that that's not the case, and then come to us.

66
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:43,600
So really it's about the limitations that the App Store and Google Play impose on what you have control over, and then how do you use that to help the algorithm?

67
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:46,640
And also the algorithms are very different, but that's secondary at this point.

68
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,320
Do you ever think about SEO optimization as part of the ASO optimization?

69
00:06:52,560 --> 00:07:03,840
So when you go into Google and you search like weather app, you know, especially if you're on a Mac platform or an iPhone directly, you're going to get as the top few listings actual links direct to the App Store generally.

70
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:12,640
Do you think about that crossover at all about how Google indexes the apps versus how the actual App Store algorithm indexes those apps?

71
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:14,560
So yes and no and maybe.

72
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:19,520
I think it's more difficult to think about how Google will index app pages specifically.

73
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:23,440
They're a lot more strict about it because it's all really templated.

74
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:32,720
But if you have a landing page for your app, an actual website for your app, that can drive a lot of traffic because that you can optimize specifically for Google.

75
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:40,000
And on the Google side of things, if you have an Android app, that does actually impact the landing page on Google Play.

76
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:42,320
So there's a lot of flexibility for Apple.

77
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:51,840
It's a lot more about the website if you end up making one, which I know as time goes on, less and less companies do or less and less companies invest in those websites.

78
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:59,040
Since we're on the topic, how much influence do links into the App Store actually have on ASO?

79
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:03,040
So, you know, like my apps over the years have been featured by TechCrunch.

80
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:07,040
They've got links in, you know, Wall Street Journal, The Verge and like all these other sites.

81
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:09,440
And then I have my own website that links to them.

82
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:18,960
Do those inbound links or Facebook sending a lot of organic traffic from shares and things like that, does that impact ASO at all to your knowledge?

83
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:20,480
Not on the App Store.

84
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:21,600
It does on Google Play.

85
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:26,560
So on Google Play, it's a big factor of what helps you go up or down in that case.

86
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,880
On the App Store, it doesn't to my knowledge.

87
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:29,280
Yeah.

88
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:35,200
So the Play Store algorithm works a lot more like the Google search algorithm.

89
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:38,160
It's a considerably more sophisticated and weird.

90
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:43,680
And what it looks at and how it reacts to different changes in apps and also in demand in general.

91
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:50,160
So one of the things I wanted to touch on is that it's hard to get that discovery attention through ASO these days.

92
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:55,840
You know, you can optimize for all the keywords you want, but pretty much every keyword is so crowded these days.

93
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:09,840
We'll talk about ways to find less crowded keywords, but what do you think are realistic expectations from ASO optimization, both for new apps and then for modifying and experimenting with ASO on an existing app?

94
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:11,440
It's an interesting question.

95
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:12,800
We'll leave it at interesting.

96
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:23,280
One of the advantages of ASO is that it's not that it's hard to get that discovery with ASO, it's that it's hard to get it without doing enough ASO.

97
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:26,320
So ASO is really a scale just like everything else.

98
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:27,680
When do you finish with a design?

99
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,120
When do you finish with code?

100
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:34,960
There's no end in most cases and you can continue to iterate and the same thing happens to ASO.

101
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:42,720
If you start with a basic set of keywords and you set it and forget it and it doesn't work, then it just won't work.

102
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:47,360
And you shouldn't really expect any sort of real return from it because you haven't really put in the time.

103
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:52,000
But as you put in more time and you start to understand specifically your app and your course.

104
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,520
And your core competitors, that's when you can start seeing real results.

105
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:59,440
And it doesn't mean that every time you make a change, you're going to see a huge impact.

106
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:07,680
But for the most part, once you start making those bigger changes, you'll see an impact that will allow you to understand, is this direction I want to be on?

107
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:11,680
And that should be more immediate and also more pronounced.

108
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:20,400
So for example, something that happens all the time is people will open the live chat on our website and ask me, why is my keyword selection not working?

109
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:22,320
And I'll say, well, is this your first experiment?

110
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:23,200
Is this your second?

111
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:23,920
Your third?

112
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:26,240
And it's usually their first or their second.

113
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,240
And they'll say, I don't think ASL works.

114
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:29,200
I don't know what to do.

115
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:30,720
The app store is broken.

116
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:36,400
And it might seem like it, and you have because you have this big expectation, but you haven't really put in the iterations.

117
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:41,360
Unless you use a tool or something else, you really won't have that intuition upfront.

118
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:56,800
So I think from an expectation standpoint, expect to spend more time on the front end thinking about things, maybe exploring the competitors, exploring opportunities, and then running four or five different iterations and then seeing actual results.

119
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:03,360
So results will come at the tail end once you've spent enough time understanding what it is you're trying to optimize for.

120
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,360
What should those expectations be?

121
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:16,880
Can someone in 2023 launch a new weather app and expect to get much traction in any relevant weather keywords with a thousand other weather apps out there?

122
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:19,760
What should expectations be going into ASO?

123
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:30,880
So I know I'm going to probably get a ton of questions after this, but the answer is yes, absolutely, you can launch an app tomorrow and expect to be in the top five results for a very successful keyword.

124
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:45,120
And on my series Keyword Teardowns, I show that almost every week there's usually an app that doesn't have the downloads, is just too new, doesn't have really any sort of popularity, yet ranks really high because everyone else is doing something wrong.

125
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:50,960
And you would expect that doing something wrong doesn't happen in important keywords, but it still does.

126
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:53,520
Maybe by 2025 that's not going to be the case.

127
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:56,000
But as of 2023, it still is.

128
00:11:56,480 --> 00:12:00,000
I looked at an important keyword this week actually on the series.

129
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:02,720
I believe I looked at investing or trading or one of those.

130
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:09,360
And you can still see issues and the biggest apps don't actually rank at the top for those reasons.

131
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:16,080
So there's still a ton of opportunity, but it's not going to be the kind of thing that you just look at it and go, hahaha, put it in your name.

132
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:18,560
And within three days, you're going to get the number one.

133
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:20,160
You're probably going to have do a lot of research.

134
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:23,200
You're probably going to have to spend some time trying things.

135
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,640
And after that, you can expect to have that sort of wind.

136
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:36,720
I talked to you a bunch of developers who read my newsletter and watch my live streams and have done this and in their own respective areas of the App Store managed to get popularity.

137
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:37,680
So it is possible.

138
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:38,400
Yeah.

139
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:43,760
And then tell me about, I know there's, from what I remember, a seven day boost for new apps.

140
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:48,400
So when you launch a one point zero, you get a seven day artificial boost.

141
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:51,200
And funny enough, I've been bugging Apple about this for years.

142
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:56,400
I think this was actually specifically on my suggestion, complaining that new apps were so disadvantaged.

143
00:12:56,880 --> 00:13:00,720
Because historically, there was a lot of weight put on the history of an app.

144
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:06,800
So the older an app was, the more kind of long term traction it had, the better chance it had at ASO.

145
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:14,080
And so Apple seems to have put this in as this artificial seven day boost to give new apps a chance.

146
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:21,840
How does that end up playing out and does it just trail off after seven days or do you see apps like stick the landing and how would they stick the landing?

147
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:30,400
So from what I've seen, and this is fairly new, not yesterday or last week, but not many, many years, what ends up happening is Apple is very lenient.

148
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:49,920
So one of the things that you see with the App Store, and I guess we'll get to that once we start talking about how the apps are actually ranking apps is that they're looking at keyword placement, meaning where you put the keyword in your app store metadata, and that could be the name, the subtitle, the keyword list, and each one of them has different impact, but also how many ratings you have.

149
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:56,640
And ratings are just a way for the algorithm to know that people care about your app, whether they like it or hate it, they still care about it in some way.

150
00:13:56,960 --> 00:13:59,600
So the boost takes away the ratings component.

151
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:02,720
You don't need to have as many ratings in order to still rank.

152
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:08,480
So they would look at the keywords that you use, and if those make sense will float you up.

153
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:09,680
And that's exactly what you want.

154
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:10,640
So that's what you want.

155
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:15,040
to take that time in the beginning to figure out your keywords a little bit better.

156
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:16,720
And that's how you stick the landing.

157
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:30,960
If you get into keywords that actually are really, really relevant for your app and your app can sell itself, meaning the icon looks good, the screenshots look good, and people do download it, those people will likely convert and go and leave a rating.

158
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:32,080
Those ratings will help.

159
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:39,600
So you're getting artificial visibility that's turning into actual downloads, actual ratings, and then the visibility will become organic.

160
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:41,120
That's kind of the idea.

161
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:46,960
You also want to make sure that you're optimizing to turn as many downloads into ratings, something I can't stress enough.

162
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:52,240
And if you do that, you would have the ability to continue and see more downloads.

163
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:57,520
And I don't know about maintaining that rank because it really depends on who else is competing for that rank.

164
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:05,360
And they might have ten X more ratings and you won't be able to compete, but then you will just go to the bottom instead of just dropping off completely.

165
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:07,200
So there are definitely ways to do it.

166
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:17,600
Even after Apple implemented that, which I think it has been like three to five years now that I first started seeing it, it's a little frustrating that, okay, I launched my one point zero.

167
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:19,680
That's like the worst the apps ever will be.

168
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,960
And so I get a boost for seven days on my one point zero.

169
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:33,360
Have you seen them do any boosts on updates or is there any other way to get a boost like that that you would get those first seven days?

170
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:46,080
So there are a bunch of boosts, but what they've also done in the process from what I've seen is they took off the really high positive impact that being around for a long time had on apps.

171
00:15:46,560 --> 00:16:01,680
So if you've been around for 14 years or 8 or 7 or 6 at this point, it doesn't make as much of a difference, which kind of offsets that a little bit, not a ton, but enough that I think it's easier to see conversion happening and to see apps shifting.

172
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:10,800
up and down, but you have a bunch of other boosts that they introduced and we all know that Apple really likes their tech and every time you use their tech, you have more potential to get featured.

173
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:12,080
It's the same with ASO.

174
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:15,840
I've looked at a few different ways in which apps will get a boost.

175
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:23,440
And again, the ratings are not going to make sense, but the app is still going to be high, so if you promote an in-purchase, you're going to get an immediate boost.

176
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:29,920
And I think now enough companies do it that it's beginning to lose its relevance, but still doesn't happen all the time.

177
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:32,720
So if you have the ability, go and do it.

178
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:38,320
It seems to work better for subscriptions than consumables in-purchases, but I've seen both work.

179
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:41,440
I know that in-app events are in the same boat.

180
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:50,160
And once news came out that in-app events help you with ranks, almost every app that I've seen had some sort of in-app event, including updates.

181
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:52,560
Apparently, Apple allowed that to happen for some reason.

182
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:54,400
So you would see a lot more of those.

183
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:59,520
There's also a case to be made for naming your in-app purchases, the promoted in-app purchases.

184
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:11,440
If you have a promoted in-app purchase that has the keyword in its name, you're also supposed to get a boost works, I would say, fifty percent of the time or depends on more factors than just the name of the in-app, but does give you a boost.

185
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:15,440
I've argued for years that apps that get featured should get a boost.

186
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,680
I've told several people at Apple this.

187
00:17:18,080 --> 00:17:20,080
I've given the feedback as much as I possibly can.

188
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:23,200
Do you see any evidence that that's happening yet?

189
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:24,240
Not as much.

190
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:31,680
It might happen, but it's just not pronounced enough that it would make a substantial difference and would kick up an app that isn't supposed to be where it is.

191
00:17:32,080 --> 00:17:34,560
Well, it took us down a rabbit hole going very deep.

192
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:36,880
Let's go back to our ASO 101.

193
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:41,920
And I wanted to talk about the key factors that impact ASO.

194
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:43,600
So I've listed some out here.

195
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:48,160
And what I'd love to do is just do a high level overview of why it matters.

196
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:54,960
And then maybe some anecdotes or maybe kind of a top two tips for each of these factors that matter for ASO.

197
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:58,240
So the first and most obvious one is the app name.

198
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:02,800
So why does the app name matter and any tips around ASO strategies for the app name?

199
00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:07,360
So the app name works in conjuncture with the subtitle and then the keyword list.

200
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:14,240
All of those I kind of see as one thing at the end of the day and what Apple does is it concatenates all of them.

201
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:21,520
It joins all of those chunks and gives value based on the position of the keyword starting from the name all the way down to the keyword list.

202
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:29,520
And the biggest tip that I can give for the name, but really all of those is take your most important keywords and put them in the beginning.

203
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:31,680
because that's where all the value is.

204
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:35,600
And you would see if you go in the app store, you'll see two schools of thought.

205
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:42,880
One is brand name, keywords describing the app, and the other is keywords describing the app, and then brand name.

206
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:54,160
The reason for doing that is you want those keywords all the way in the beginning as early as possible because those are going to get all the value, all the good juice from the algorithm.

207
00:18:54,320 --> 00:19:00,640
So whatever keywords you think are most relevant for your app, stick them in the beginning, whether before the brand name or after.

208
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:09,200
That's kind of a personal choice, depends on how you want to brand your app, but that's the best way to do it and extending that to the subtitle.

209
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:13,280
You want to continue and place your keywords in order of importance.

210
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:17,840
And so earlier in the subtitle is still better than the end of the subtitle.

211
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:19,360
Now, there is a catch.

212
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:22,240
I discovered this bug in the algorithm.

213
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:23,440
It's really strange.

214
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:24,880
I can't really explain it.

215
00:19:25,040 --> 00:19:27,120
I can explain it, but it doesn't make any sense.

216
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:28,800
Apple hasn't told me anything about it.

217
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:31,120
So I don't know if they're going to fix it, if they have fixed it.

218
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:32,560
I haven't seen it being fixed.

219
00:19:33,280 --> 00:19:43,360
So what it is, and this is very technical, if you're using characters that are not alphanumeric, so an ampersand or one of those characters, even a dash in some cases, if it's one of those special dashes.

220
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:47,200
It takes up two characters as far as the algorithm can see.

221
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:50,400
It's a Unicode thing.

222
00:19:51,040 --> 00:19:51,680
That makes sense.

223
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:52,640
Exactly.

224
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:59,520
So what ends up happening is you can feed it more than thirty characters in App Store Connect.

225
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:05,760
So your name is less than thirty characters, but the algorithm truncates it after thirty total characters.

226
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:12,640
And so if you have Unicode characters that extend beyond what you just do is you cut the last keyword.

227
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:16,240
And so you'll come across situations where an app has the perfect name.

228
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:17,760
This happens in recipes.

229
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:22,320
I believe I did an app tear down and a keyword tear down on one of those keywords.

230
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:24,960
And I saw that one of the apps was doing that exactly thing.

231
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:28,320
So if you look for the keyword and cut it in half, they're number one.

232
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:32,720
But if you look for the proper keyword, they're not even in the selection.

233
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:33,920
So that's great.

234
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:36,880
What I usually say is try not to use those characters.

235
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:39,440
And I think in general, they're not really helpful in the name.

236
00:20:39,680 --> 00:20:41,120
You want to keep it as concise as possible.

237
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:54,640
But if you do, aim for something like twenty seven characters total out of the thirty or twenty six, even just to be on the safe side and not have anything cut off or put a keyword at the end that you don't care about, but you should never do that because that's wasteful.

238
00:20:54,960 --> 00:21:04,560
I like that you lumped name subtitle and keywords all into one big category because they all essentially just have different weight but have similar impact.

239
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:07,520
Does the order of the keywords also matter?

240
00:21:07,760 --> 00:21:10,880
Like keywords earlier in your keywords matter less.

241
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:11,680
Absolutely.

242
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:12,400
Oh, wow.

243
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:14,400
And that's because they're all joined together.

244
00:21:15,120 --> 00:21:17,760
So they're all one long set of keywords.

245
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,960
And there are many ways to optimize your keyword list.

246
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:22,880
You don't need to have repetition.

247
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:24,320
You don't need to have quotes.

248
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:26,720
You don't need to have two word keywords.

249
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:38,720
You can slim them all down because what the algorithm will do, especially because they're all combined, the name, the subtitle and the keyword list is it will start combining keywords from all three and rank you for those.

250
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:47,440
But because they come with different algorithm juju, you're going to have different variety of where the algorithm will fit you.

251
00:21:47,680 --> 00:22:03,760
So, for example, if a keyword from the beginning of your name and the end of your keyword list are joined together and the algorithm believes that it's going to be good for you, it'll probably have the lowest score of all combinations because it touches the end of the keyword list.

252
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:10,240
And so where you put those keywords also matters even when they're combined with other keywords in the name and the subtitle.

253
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:20,880
And another thing that's really important, and I call that the cardinal sin of ASO for anyone who's read anything that I've written, and I write that all the time, is duplication is evil.

254
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:25,200
And the reason it's evil is Apple doesn't penalize you for anything of that.

255
00:22:25,360 --> 00:22:28,000
They're not going to say, When you duplicate, you're trying to cheat us.

256
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:28,640
Not at all.

257
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:36,880
But because of what I just explained, how they combine words and give them the least juju based on where they are, you end up losing.

258
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:42,720
So when you duplicate, they ignore the first dimensions of the keyword and look for the last dimension of the keyword.

259
00:22:42,960 --> 00:22:43,920
And that's what happens.

260
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:51,440
So you take a keyword from the beginning of the name, the first word in your name, and you duplicateated the end of your keyword list and you ruined everything.

261
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:52,560
Gotcha.

262
00:22:52,960 --> 00:22:54,880
Let's do a little live keyword help.

263
00:22:55,360 --> 00:22:57,760
I am releasing a big update to my weather app.

264
00:22:58,160 --> 00:23:03,840
So the name of the app is Weather Up, and we're releasing a big update with some really cool widgets.

265
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,080
That's going to be the main value prop is the widgets and the Apple Watch complications.

266
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:18,480
So following this like line of thinking and to get really practical, weather up dash widgets, or should it be weather widgets by weather up?

267
00:23:18,720 --> 00:23:20,160
But then I'm duplicating a keyword.

268
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:30,960
So that's not good is having the up in between weather up widgets and complications or whatever, you know, the rest of the title is, does it get penalized for having the up in the middle?

269
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:32,240
Well, so that depends.

270
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,120
Is it weather up a single word or weather space up?

271
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:36,480
It's weather space up.

272
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:41,600
That's much better because you don't really want the algorithm to try and split up words and it's not going to do it well.

273
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:51,600
So you already have weather in your name, which means weather up dash or colon really anything is going to be your best bet by far because it's weather.

274
00:23:51,760 --> 00:23:52,720
That's exactly what you want.

275
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:54,800
The real question is what do you want to optimize for?

276
00:23:54,960 --> 00:24:07,680
And it's funny because I just sent out a newsletter with my weekly recap of what's new in apps and one of the trends that's happening right now is is weather apps are getting more downloads because Apple weather went down and everyone is looking at it.

277
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:14,080
But I'm sure even for the next few weeks, people are not going to think, oh yeah, Apple's going to remain up forever.

278
00:24:14,240 --> 00:24:14,880
It's going to be okay.

279
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:16,400
And they're still going to look for more apps.

280
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:18,080
So you're probably in a good position.

281
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:24,160
So I would do weather up colon or dash and then whatever else you want to optimize for.

282
00:24:24,320 --> 00:24:33,200
So if it's widgets, you can come up with something that would take widgets and make them unique beyond the weather widgets because, and we'd have to look this up and see what the data says.

283
00:24:33,600 --> 00:24:42,480
Another problem or another challenge that many don't actually see initially is what your gut says because weather widget sounds like a great keyword, but is it really?

284
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:43,840
It's hard to say.

285
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:45,120
We're not the algorithm.

286
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:47,680
We don't exist in the App Store the way the App Store is.

287
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:52,240
And we do have that data directly from Apple that can help us guide our decision.

288
00:24:52,640 --> 00:24:53,760
We should always start with that.

289
00:24:53,920 --> 00:24:55,200
And that's the popularity score.

290
00:24:55,920 --> 00:25:00,880
So I would go in and look for we have a tool called Keyword Inspector does exactly what you think.

291
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:11,120
You put in a keyword and we'll give you one, all the results from right now, but also a few more intelligence-based features including the popularity, how competitive it is and some other numbers.

292
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:12,720
And based on that, you can see.

293
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:19,280
So maybe real-time alerts because we know the algorithm is going to combine whether with real time and with widgets.

294
00:25:19,680 --> 00:25:24,640
So maybe real time widgets, real time alerts, something to that extent is where I would start at least.

295
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:25,200
Got it.

296
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:26,720
Cunning fact about widgets.

297
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:34,160
I know that for a long time having widgets in your app name was actually pushing apps down and having them in the subtitle was okay.

298
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:36,960
That was about a year and a half ago, two years ago.

299
00:25:37,120 --> 00:25:38,240
I don't know if that's still the case.

300
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:38,640
Yeah.

301
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:42,240
I don't come across many apps that have widgets in the name anymore, probably for that reason.

302
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:50,640
I'm glad I asked that because it's a perfect example where intuition might actually not be a great ASO strategy.

303
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:52,960
The real answer would be first to find out.

304
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:56,560
if weather widgets is even a popular keyword that people are searching.

305
00:25:56,800 --> 00:26:06,320
And then once we know whether it's popular or not, then we need to look at whether or not my app would have a chance of ranking for weather widgets.

306
00:26:06,560 --> 00:26:06,880
Exactly.

307
00:26:07,200 --> 00:26:19,360
So even though weather widgets is what I'm excited about and what I think the value prop of the app is, it might not actually make for a great title and a great title keyword depending on all these other factors.

308
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:29,600
Those are the kind of thought processes and why app figures and other ASO tools are so important when you're doing ASO is because your intuition on what's going to work.

309
00:26:30,800 --> 00:26:37,440
And what you should title and not title often doesn't work out and doesn't actually drive the downloads and the discovery you were hoping for.

310
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:37,920
Yep.

311
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:41,040
If you only trust intuition, you probably won't see results.

312
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:47,520
And fun fact, I just looked up weather widgets and it's a tiny popularity score, so it's not worth optimizing for.

313
00:26:48,080 --> 00:26:48,240
Wow.

314
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:49,200
See?

315
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:51,680
Yeah, totally not what I would have guessed.

316
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:56,160
So I would go for something like real-time alerts and continue from there.

317
00:26:56,480 --> 00:26:58,800
There's a lot of real-time weather, weather alerts.

318
00:26:59,200 --> 00:27:00,240
All those are really good.

319
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:01,440
Fascinating.

320
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:03,760
I took us down another rabbit hole.

321
00:27:03,920 --> 00:27:05,760
Let's get back to our SO 101.

322
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:09,120
So we've talked about app names, subtitles and keywords.

323
00:27:09,440 --> 00:27:13,680
The next more important thing for ASO is screenshots and video previews.

324
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:16,000
Tell me about optimizing for those.

325
00:27:16,960 --> 00:27:31,120
So that's the second part of abstract optimization because ultimately what we started talking about before abstract optimization is you want to get the algorithm to float your app up, but you also need people to understand what they're looking at and then get it.

326
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:36,080
And the real reason for that is there's just so many apps that do what people want at this point.

327
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:42,240
There are not many niches where you only have one app that is just perfect and everything else is not exactly what it is.

328
00:27:42,560 --> 00:27:59,440
And so it's very easy to just swipe back and forth between different apps if an app doesn't really capture your attention immediately, and especially today because many of the apps are free or free with a trial or free with an in-app purchase, it's so much easier to just try the one that fits best.

329
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:04,960
And if it doesn't work out, then throw it out, try another one, but by that time you kind of lose unless you're that app.

330
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:06,480
So you want to be that app.

331
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:16,720
And so the screenshots are the biggest thing on the app page, and you really want to capture their attention in a very similar way to how you come up with keywords.

332
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:20,160
For example, we know that people are not looking for weather widgets.

333
00:28:20,240 --> 00:28:23,600
We just found out, but we know that they're looking for weather alerts.

334
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,080
Popularity of 31, much better.

335
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:27,360
I just looked it up too.

336
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:33,600
And if you know that, now you know that you can capture their intent also through the screenshots.

337
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:43,440
So if you show what the keyword says and you put the keyword on the screen, the odds of them looking at the screen and saying, hmm, I don't know if this is for me really, really diminish.

338
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:45,840
And instead it's, oh, that's exactly what I was looking for.

339
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:46,960
Yes, I'll give it a try.

340
00:28:47,440 --> 00:28:57,520
And so you want your keyword, you want your entire metadata, which is the way the icon looks, the name, the subtitle, the keyword list, your videos or your screenshots and your screenshots.

341
00:28:58,080 --> 00:29:01,680
And even your description to some extent, but I think fewer people actually read these days.

342
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:03,600
So it's all the visuals.

343
00:29:04,400 --> 00:29:08,400
You want them to have the same messaging and you want that messaging to be very pleasant.

344
00:29:08,560 --> 00:29:10,160
And there are many different ways to test it.

345
00:29:10,640 --> 00:29:21,200
I had on my show on AFChats a guy who does AB testing, ran AB tests for Google, and he went through his entire process of AB testing screenshots, especially across localizations.

346
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:23,200
It was really, really amazing.

347
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:28,160
Definitely worth a watch to see just the thought process behind it and the expectation behind it.

348
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:37,760
And he was sharing information about how he ran tests for other companies that in one case he just couldn't get to something that worked and they tried eighteen different tests or something crazy like that.

349
00:29:38,080 --> 00:29:39,920
It's wild and eventually he got results.

350
00:29:40,080 --> 00:29:50,880
So it's more about the thinking of how do I take the whole brand basically or the whole intent and express it and then use colors and then use visuals and then use a video.

351
00:29:51,440 --> 00:29:52,880
A video may not actually help.

352
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:56,320
That's also something that came up in one of our AF chats.

353
00:29:56,880 --> 00:30:01,280
Some people don't like seeing videos that are kind of fluffy and markety and some people do.

354
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:02,880
So it really depends on your audience.

355
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:10,720
But I would say spend as much time as you do on finding keywords on using those keywords beyond just in your text metadata.

356
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:11,920
That makes a ton of sense.

357
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:21,520
And then before we hit record, you're actually sharing an anecdote that some engineer at Apple told somebody that they were indexing words that were in screenshots.

358
00:30:22,400 --> 00:30:22,960
Is that true?

359
00:30:23,840 --> 00:30:24,800
Not from my experience.

360
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:27,040
And I really want that to be true.

361
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:27,840
The technology is there.

362
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:31,520
We obviously we know if we use an iPhone and the technology is magical.

363
00:30:31,760 --> 00:30:33,200
So I would imagine it would be right.

364
00:30:33,840 --> 00:30:39,120
Either all the time or most of the time because those are designers' screenshots.

365
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:45,600
Those are not just pictures of signs that we found on the street where the iPhone does a really good job at extracting those words too.

366
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:47,920
So I totally see that as plausible.

367
00:30:48,080 --> 00:30:48,560
I haven't seen.

368
00:30:48,560 --> 00:30:55,520
I have not seen any sort of indication that it's actually happening from all the keyword tear downs that I do.

369
00:30:55,600 --> 00:30:57,200
And I do a keyword tear down every week.

370
00:30:57,200 --> 00:30:59,680
So I see a ton of different apps and a ton of different keywords.

371
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:03,040
And I always try to reverse engineer how they got to where they are.

372
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:10,720
People also ask me all the time what I think about specific keywords, and I've yet to see one keyword where the screenshots actually align with that.

373
00:31:10,960 --> 00:31:11,520
I'm waiting.

374
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:15,520
So if there is an example, I would love to see it, but I haven't seen it yet.

375
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:17,360
Maybe that's in the future.

376
00:31:17,920 --> 00:31:18,000
Okay.

377
00:31:18,320 --> 00:31:22,320
The last one on our key factors list are ratings and reviews.

378
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:26,800
How do ratings and reviews impact ASO and ASO and discoverability.

379
00:31:27,360 --> 00:31:36,160
So if there's something to take from this, from this ASO 101, 101 that we're trying to maintain, is that reviews don't matter and ratings definitely matter.

380
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:38,480
And that's something that I get asked all the time.

381
00:31:39,200 --> 00:31:44,640
Most people don't believe me when I say if you have more ratings, you're going to beat apps that have more downloads.

382
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:47,280
That's just the way it is on the App Store.

383
00:31:47,600 --> 00:31:50,640
And every week I have a guide that shows it in some capacity.

384
00:31:51,280 --> 00:32:02,240
Because what it is, if you think about it, is if your app is popular, if people care about your app, whether it's a negative rating or a positive rating, but they care about your app, that's what the algorithm is trying to continue and promote.

385
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:13,120
They care less about the downloads because those you can buy or those you can get in a bazillion different ways, including Apple search ads or any other ad network, but the ratings feel a lot more organic.

386
00:32:13,520 --> 00:32:15,440
You can also buy ratings and you shouldn't.

387
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:26,320
There are many bad things that can happen, but let's say in a world where that's not the case, ratings are the more organic way of showing Apple that people care about your app, so the algorithm should.

388
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:37,840
And one of the measures that I look at, I don't know if this is beyond the one hundred and eleven, but that's something that I look at a lot is what's the ability of every competitor that I have to turn a download into a rating.

389
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:41,280
And I call that number the DPR, the DPR, the downloads per rating metric.

390
00:32:41,680 --> 00:32:46,160
And the idea is how many downloads do you need to produce one rating?

391
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:54,240
And you can see that when you look at Keyword Inspector and you can see apps that are doing really well and that number is really low versus apps that are not doing really well.

392
00:32:54,640 --> 00:32:56,400
And that's how you know you can beat them.

393
00:32:56,960 --> 00:33:02,640
So if they have 1000 new ratings and you're trying to think, oh, how many downloads do I need to get to 1000 ratings?

394
00:33:03,040 --> 00:33:07,360
You have those numbers to really give you your own algorithm for figuring that out.

395
00:33:07,680 --> 00:33:10,400
And then you can go out and you can promote your app and push it to that point.

396
00:33:10,560 --> 00:33:15,200
And then once it's there, it will get the visibility and hopefully that will be organic enough and you maintain that position.

397
00:33:15,760 --> 00:33:16,080
Very cool.

398
00:33:16,640 --> 00:33:17,600
Those are some great tips.

399
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:25,040
You said reviews don't matter, but they don't matter to the algorithm, but they matter to the people's side.

400
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:25,600
Of course.

401
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:36,880
So people do probably read reviews and if it's all one star reviews and then you still have a four and a half star rating, probably would detract from downloads.

402
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:38,000
That's very possible.

403
00:33:38,480 --> 00:33:44,640
I think the point at which people make the decision to get, doesn't always require scrolling to the point where you see the reviews.

404
00:33:45,200 --> 00:33:45,360
Right.

405
00:33:45,520 --> 00:33:46,080
That's a good point.

406
00:33:46,560 --> 00:33:48,480
And so it's kind of a yes or no or maybe.

407
00:33:48,560 --> 00:33:50,480
I think if you have the right average, you'll be okay.

408
00:33:50,640 --> 00:34:17,920
And I've talked to Costa, the app hunter, who pointed out that there are a lot of scams out there that buy a lot of ratings, enough ratings to have a very high average and also a very high count so they can get those ranks, but they have a very poor average if you only look at the ratings that come from reviews and people don't really have a big problem continuing to download them because they don't scroll to that point, so they don't see all the one star reviews that say scam, scam, scam, we'll take your money and I'll give you value.

409
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:20,320
So I think it's more about the average and the counts.

410
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:21,600
Yeah, that makes sense.

411
00:34:21,920 --> 00:34:25,440
What is kind of the minimum average you see driving good downloads?

412
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:27,760
Is there four point five, four point four?

413
00:34:28,160 --> 00:34:28,640
I don't know.

414
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:30,480
Is this just like the more ratings?

415
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:31,760
actually help?

416
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:35,360
Like, I mean, the higher the rating actually helped drive more downloads?

417
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:36,240
I don't think so.

418
00:34:36,480 --> 00:34:37,920
I think there's a minimum for both.

419
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:44,000
If you see an app that has a handful of ratings and maybe a 3.8 average, you're not going to take it seriously.

420
00:34:44,160 --> 00:34:52,320
But if you see an app with a 4.2 and 1000 ratings, you're probably going to be okay and it's not going to play as much of a role in you getting it or not getting it.

421
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:55,040
So I think you have to hit that minimum and then you'll be fine.

422
00:34:55,440 --> 00:34:55,520
Yeah.

423
00:34:55,840 --> 00:35:03,360
Sometimes when I see an app that's like a five-star rating or four-point-9-star rating, I kind of wonder like, oh, geez, are they buying ratings or something?

424
00:35:03,680 --> 00:35:12,000
Because I mean, I have seen a lot of apps get that organically, but yeah., I wonder if there's also like a too high of rating where people start to question whether it's real or not.

425
00:35:12,240 --> 00:35:12,720
I don't know.

426
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:14,160
I feel like that's a developer thing.

427
00:35:14,320 --> 00:35:16,160
Most people just think, oh, this is an amazing app.

428
00:35:16,640 --> 00:35:17,280
Sounds good to me.

429
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:17,600
Okay.

430
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:23,360
So we already hit the biggest mistake in ASO, which is duplicating keywords.

431
00:35:23,680 --> 00:35:26,080
Any other common ASO mistakes that we should hit on?

432
00:35:26,800 --> 00:35:29,200
So we hit on multiple ASO mistakes actually.

433
00:35:29,600 --> 00:35:39,760
One is duplication, and that's really the ultimate sin because it ruins everything in a way that's very hard to detect, but also not looking at popularity scores and trusting your instincts.

434
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:43,200
That's another huge mistake that can lead you down a very bad path.

435
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:44,800
And it's really easy to do.

436
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:45,600
It takes seconds.

437
00:35:45,680 --> 00:35:47,120
It took me a second to do that just now.

438
00:35:47,360 --> 00:35:49,600
That's another one of those things that I would say do first.

439
00:35:50,240 --> 00:35:54,880
The other is not actually looking at your app's name as a place to put keywords.

440
00:35:55,200 --> 00:35:56,320
That's always, always bad.

441
00:35:56,560 --> 00:36:01,520
There are still some companies that believe in some indies that believe that the name has to be clean.

442
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:02,480
That's not true.

443
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:04,880
I mean, if you want to get downloads, that's not true.

444
00:36:05,040 --> 00:36:07,920
And that's something that just changed over time.

445
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:18,720
There used to be an ASO hack where if your app name was an exact string match for a popular keyword, like my mirror app, I actually used this.

446
00:36:19,360 --> 00:36:21,040
My mirror app was mirror.

447
00:36:21,760 --> 00:36:25,920
and a Unicode symbol and it was an exact match for mirror.

448
00:36:26,160 --> 00:36:32,240
And apparently tens of thousands of people were searching mirror every day because I was getting like four or five thousand downloads a day.

449
00:36:32,880 --> 00:36:33,600
Does that still work?

450
00:36:33,920 --> 00:36:42,000
Is there still that kind of exact string match bonus on the app name if you absolutely do have like a very popular keyword?

451
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:42,640
Yeah.

452
00:36:42,880 --> 00:36:44,000
So it's really the focus.

453
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:50,720
Focus is how I call wanting to think about the algorithm understanding you as doing multiple things or few things.

454
00:36:51,200 --> 00:36:54,640
And the fewer things you do, the more it likes you for those fewer things.

455
00:36:55,280 --> 00:36:59,520
So by having only one word in your app name, you're really focusing.

456
00:36:59,840 --> 00:37:04,320
And yeah, unfortunately, that trick still works and there are some apps that continue to take advantage of it.

457
00:37:04,480 --> 00:37:05,360
And it's pretty ugly.

458
00:37:05,920 --> 00:37:15,280
I don't like to think of it as a trick that companies should use or people should use because I feel like eventually it will stop working and then you just have a name with a funky character, but it definitely still works.

459
00:37:15,760 --> 00:37:17,360
I did that a decade ago.

460
00:37:17,680 --> 00:37:18,400
It's one of those things.

461
00:37:18,560 --> 00:37:20,720
I mean, yeah, I felt like, is this just a hack?

462
00:37:21,120 --> 00:37:29,440
But when app store search is so broken that other apps are using those kinds of tricks, you're just shooting yourself in the foot if you're not taking advantage.

463
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:35,440
And ultimately, my app was a really great, it actually, I think still to this day, has like four point nine stars.

464
00:37:35,440 --> 00:37:42,160
4.9 stars and is a really great app to be a mirror, which is it's such a simple thing.

465
00:37:42,400 --> 00:37:46,080
And so that's my moral justification for having done it myself.

466
00:37:46,320 --> 00:37:48,560
But the funny thing is, I thought the same thing.

467
00:37:48,720 --> 00:37:51,200
I thought, uh, any day now, Apple is going to like fix this.

468
00:37:51,360 --> 00:37:53,680
And it's been a decade and they haven't fixed it yet.

469
00:37:54,480 --> 00:37:58,800
But it does seem like that's actually been less of a boost than it was in the past.

470
00:37:59,040 --> 00:38:04,720
Because like five or ten years ago, it would just like rocket you to the top of like really popular keywords.

471
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:06,560
And now it's like a boost.

472
00:38:06,880 --> 00:38:15,600
And maybe that kind of thinking about it as the focus kind of explains how it helps, but it's not to just like a rocket ship to the top of a popular keyword.

473
00:38:15,840 --> 00:38:20,880
It's not a hardcoded rule that if the name matches the keyword, just number one automatically.

474
00:38:21,280 --> 00:38:24,640
And there's still other factors that play into this, like ratings and other things.

475
00:38:25,040 --> 00:38:32,240
And I think a lot more apps are at the point where they're doing enough to really help the algorithm understand what they do.

476
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:36,880
I'm not going to go full out and say all the apps do ASO because it's absolutely not true.

477
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:40,880
But enough apps do the kind of start with ASO.

478
00:38:41,520 --> 00:38:45,120
And so there's still more to just the keywords at this point.

479
00:38:45,440 --> 00:38:50,720
And that's also why more companies need to do ASO and more people need to do ASO because everyone else is doing it.

480
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:53,920
Yeah, you're shooting yourself in the foot if you're not paying attention to ASO.

481
00:38:54,560 --> 00:39:04,080
If you're a big app and you don't have a team paying attention to this kind of stuff or at least one person who thinks about this day in and day out, you're wasting potential organic traffic.

482
00:39:04,480 --> 00:39:13,680
So you said that not having keywords in your app name is a common mistake, but then we said that there's this element of focus.

483
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:22,720
When do you think it is actually appropriate to have a short name to establish that focus only when your brand is very keywordy?

484
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:24,160
It really depends.

485
00:39:24,480 --> 00:39:26,480
When I say you need to have keywordss in your name.

486
00:39:26,560 --> 00:39:40,880
It doesn't mean that you need to take up all thirty characters or twenty seven if you're using any of those ASCII characters we mentioned before, but it just means that you need to think about it from an explanatory standpoint and not just have your brand name as the only keyword, as the only text in the name.

487
00:39:41,200 --> 00:39:46,720
You can have a little bit more than that, and that little bit more is what we can stretch with focusing around focusing.

488
00:39:47,440 --> 00:39:56,640
Ideally, you wouldn't have to focus too much because then you're really siloing yourself, unless you're in a niche where there's really just one or maybe two keywords that matter.

489
00:39:57,200 --> 00:40:03,040
And that does exist, especially around games, but not as much for almost any other app.

490
00:40:03,840 --> 00:40:20,800
So from my experience, you want to focus when you're either very new to a category and the keyword is very competitive and you just want to take all the algorithm juice and focus it on this one keyword and later on with more ratings, with more downloads, you'll be able to attack those other keyword combinations.

491
00:40:21,360 --> 00:40:22,880
That's usually what I go for.

492
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:31,280
If I'm very new or I'm working with an app that is kind of weak or hasn't done any ASO for a long time and the competition is fierce, that's where you want to focus.

493
00:40:31,920 --> 00:40:33,520
A good example would be Dropbox.

494
00:40:33,840 --> 00:40:38,080
So people who are searching Dropbox, they're going to show up high for Dropbox.

495
00:40:38,400 --> 00:40:39,040
It's a brand name.

496
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:41,040
They're going to be the number one result.

497
00:40:41,200 --> 00:40:45,040
They're not going to lose that number one result by moving the keyword to the side.

498
00:40:45,840 --> 00:40:56,800
And so by putting, I don't remember exactly what it was, but like photo backup by Dropbox or something like that, then they still have their branded keyword there or their brand name in the title.

499
00:40:57,360 --> 00:41:01,920
And then they have a really heavy focus on whatever it was, like photo backups.

500
00:41:02,320 --> 00:41:06,080
And so the focus is on photo backups as the first two keywords.

501
00:41:06,320 --> 00:41:06,640
Exactly.

502
00:41:06,880 --> 00:41:08,080
And then their brand is right there.

503
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:11,200
And so I think, if I remember correctly, that was it.

504
00:41:11,760 --> 00:41:15,040
And so they weren't using, like you said, they weren't using all 27 characters.

505
00:41:15,280 --> 00:41:20,160
It was a very focused name on that really important keyword that they were going after.

506
00:41:20,640 --> 00:41:20,880
Exactly.

507
00:41:21,120 --> 00:41:22,480
And it made perfect sense to me.

508
00:41:22,800 --> 00:41:24,320
I think it was totally the right move.

509
00:41:24,480 --> 00:41:27,520
And I think they undid it a few weeks later and I thought that was a mistake.

510
00:41:27,840 --> 00:41:46,480
Maybe they got some of the juice and then because isn't there a little bit of like training the algorithm that you're good for certain keywords and then you So let's say they did that for a month and then the algorithm learns that they're a really good result for photo backup and then they move it back to the right side of the brand name.

511
00:41:47,280 --> 00:41:50,560
They would still potentially maintain a high ranking for those keywords, right?

512
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:50,960
Yeah.

513
00:41:51,280 --> 00:41:58,880
So even if they moved it into the keyword list or the subtitle, they would still have that sort of impact, but other apps are also competing on this.

514
00:41:59,040 --> 00:42:04,480
So if other apps are actively optimizing for it, that learning will diminish over time.

515
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:08,240
And the more active the keyword is, the faster that happens.

516
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:09,760
So you can do it.

517
00:42:09,920 --> 00:42:17,680
And in a if your niche specifically doesn't have that sort of churn or ASO experimentation by competitors, it's probably going to be okay.

518
00:42:17,920 --> 00:42:23,360
And I've seen that more three years ago than what I see now, but it's still a thing.

519
00:42:23,840 --> 00:42:26,240
It's just because everyone else is moving forward.

520
00:42:26,640 --> 00:42:29,120
The algorithm just can't hold on to you.

521
00:42:29,280 --> 00:42:30,560
Another mistake that people make.

522
00:42:30,560 --> 00:42:37,040
People make is they don't look at ASO as something that's me against the competitors, because that's really what it is.

523
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:39,360
It's not us against the algorithm.

524
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:40,960
It's not us against the App Store.

525
00:42:41,360 --> 00:42:43,680
It's us against whoever else is competing with us.

526
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:53,120
And that doesn't even end up being the same set of competitors you think of for your app as a developer, as a CO, or as a product owner, because the features don't have to be the same.

527
00:42:53,440 --> 00:42:55,200
The algorithm has to think they're the same.

528
00:42:55,920 --> 00:43:00,640
Those are your real, I call them ASO competitors, but those are your real competitors in that sense.

529
00:43:01,120 --> 00:43:07,840
So if they're doing better than you, whatever it is, more ratings, a better conversion rate, anything like that, they're going to rank you.

530
00:43:08,320 --> 00:43:11,440
And those are the ones you have to beat, not really anyone else.

531
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:15,680
That's why ASO isn't a set it and forget it play.

532
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:19,200
You need to actually iterate and keep an eye.

533
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:30,880
And, you know, if you're Dropbox and you put photo backup as your first keyword and you started ranking really high for that, then you need to watch that for the next six months and make sure that when you move it, you don't trail off over time.

534
00:43:31,200 --> 00:43:34,560
And then, yeah, look at the competitor doesn't slip in there.

535
00:43:34,960 --> 00:43:35,040
Yeah.

536
00:43:35,360 --> 00:43:35,600
Right.

537
00:43:36,240 --> 00:43:42,080
So we touched on it already, but I did want to do a quick overview of keyword research.

538
00:43:42,560 --> 00:43:50,240
So how do you discover?, implement, iterate on the keywords that are actually going to matter for your app.

539
00:43:51,120 --> 00:43:57,680
I wrote a few guides that go through really all of those things in a lot of detail, and then one that has an overview of all of them.

540
00:43:58,160 --> 00:43:59,920
So those are great reading materials.

541
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:13,200
But if you think about keyword research is you want to identify keywords where your app can compete, which means the number of ratings that you're getting or you're planning to get if you're going to do any kind of push, kind of aligned with the top five results.

542
00:44:13,440 --> 00:44:15,200
I think that's the easiest way to look at it.

543
00:44:15,600 --> 00:44:16,160
That's number one.

544
00:44:16,560 --> 00:44:32,160
You have to find keywords Teardown, you'll see what I'm talking about, where some apps unintuitively optimize for keywords they believe make sense, but without looking at the data.

545
00:44:32,880 --> 00:44:36,160
And so there could be keywords that are very similar that are actually better.

546
00:44:36,960 --> 00:44:40,720
And if you optimize for those, you'll see that because they're not optimizing for them.

547
00:44:40,960 --> 00:44:45,360
And another thing that you'll see when doing ASO is most apps try to copy other apps.

548
00:44:45,760 --> 00:44:52,880
And so if one does it incorrectly but convincingly enough, almost everyone else will try to copy it in that particular niche.

549
00:44:53,040 --> 00:44:58,080
And that's really a great opportunity because you can just do it right and then you rank all of them.

550
00:44:58,560 --> 00:45:00,000
So you want to find those keywords.

551
00:45:00,320 --> 00:45:05,360
The best ways that I have to find those keywords are, one, look at competitors.

552
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:09,200
We have a report called Competitors Keywords that does exactly that.

553
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:11,360
We try to name everything the way it should be.

554
00:45:11,520 --> 00:45:18,880
You put in your competitors and it will tell you what keywords they're using and where they're seeing success and where they're not seeing success.

555
00:45:19,120 --> 00:45:21,680
And you can use both of those to your advantage.

556
00:45:22,320 --> 00:45:40,000
I spoke to Ian from the BBC on one of my AF chats who said he used that report to find a different idea for a different way to say a word that because his app was just so much stronger than all the other apps using it, but he just didn't think about it., once he started using it, he immediately saw results, which was just amazing.

557
00:45:40,640 --> 00:45:42,320
So that's my go to tool.

558
00:45:42,560 --> 00:45:51,680
I look at what they do and I try to find ones that I don't use or that I use but I'm not ranking high enough in, which means the algorithm knows me already and I just need to do some tweaking.

559
00:45:51,840 --> 00:45:56,000
We have an AI based system that generates keywords, not chat GPT stuff.

560
00:45:56,160 --> 00:45:58,720
We had this before, but it's actually a lot simpler.

561
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:00,400
It doesn't guess in any way.

562
00:46:00,560 --> 00:46:12,320
It just looks at all the other apps that are competitive and are popular in your niche and it extracts their keywords and continues to do a few iterations on those to find keywords that are really relevant to one specific keyword.

563
00:46:12,800 --> 00:46:15,920
And those give you a lot of ideas for things that you can use.

564
00:46:16,320 --> 00:46:18,160
We have that in many different flavors.

565
00:46:18,320 --> 00:46:19,280
And so you can select.

566
00:46:19,360 --> 00:46:21,760
And then we have filters that you can apply on top of it.

567
00:46:22,080 --> 00:46:32,320
But at the end of the day, the one that I also like is I love to read competitor reviews and to see what their users are saying, because that is their intent, and we talked about intent before.

568
00:46:32,480 --> 00:46:34,400
Someone is coming into the App Store to find something.

569
00:46:35,040 --> 00:46:36,720
They're not looking to read an article.

570
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:39,440
Even though those exist, they have a problem.

571
00:46:39,840 --> 00:46:40,560
They need a solution.

572
00:46:40,960 --> 00:46:46,800
And usually in reviews, you'll find those same words that identify intent from competitors.

573
00:46:47,040 --> 00:46:48,480
Take those and you're golden.

574
00:46:48,880 --> 00:46:49,280
That's great.

575
00:46:49,840 --> 00:46:51,200
And then what's the strategy?

576
00:46:51,360 --> 00:47:00,080
I mean, we've touched on it a little bit, but when you find a keyword that you could potentially rank higher in, how do you rank higher?

577
00:47:00,400 --> 00:47:05,760
Is that where you actually move it up and you experiment with putting that in your title or subtitle?

578
00:47:06,240 --> 00:47:12,640
And a lot of the experimentation around better ranking is actually just moving keywords around and adding new keywords.

579
00:47:13,360 --> 00:47:14,080
In a sense, yeah.

580
00:47:14,720 --> 00:47:21,600
If you know that the keyword list, the name and the subtitle are all joined together, you have a lot of characters to play with.

581
00:47:21,920 --> 00:47:23,440
And we know that placement is important.

582
00:47:24,240 --> 00:47:26,320
You know that where you put it makes a big difference.

583
00:47:26,960 --> 00:47:41,920
And so depending on if it's a primary keyword, meaning it's a very core keyword that you have to be ranked in, maybe a secondary keyword, meaning a keyword that when added to a primary keyword gives you a good combination, that's how you decide where to put it.

584
00:47:42,400 --> 00:47:48,640
And the more core the keyword is, the earlier in this long string that it should be, because it will combine with everything else.

585
00:47:48,960 --> 00:47:53,760
It will combine in any case, but you want that good juju starting from left to right.

586
00:47:54,160 --> 00:47:57,840
And the way I like to think about it is I like to think about it as themes or sets.

587
00:47:58,480 --> 00:48:02,960
You want to use a set that works very well together for the name, the subtitle and the keyword list.

588
00:48:03,120 --> 00:48:11,280
list and what I do is either try a new set for an experiment or I try to tweak a set by moving things around just a little bit.

589
00:48:11,600 --> 00:48:13,680
And that's optimization in a nutshell.

590
00:48:13,840 --> 00:48:15,200
